  "image_name": "0ec661d0da6e49751af49aba16e848fd.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how influenza viral ribonucleoprotein complexes (vRNPs) enter the nucleus and use host nuclear machinery to transcribe and process viral mRNAs. Viral polymerase subunits associate with import factors and move into the nucleus, where host proteins such as ANP32A appear to support assembly of a complementary RNP (cRNP) and then mature vRNPs. NP is added to the viral RNA with the help of loading factors, while NEP and NS1 are shown as additional viral regulators.\n\nA central theme is the close coupling between viral transcription and host chromatin-associated RNA polymerase II. The viral polymerase interacts with Pol II and its CTD, and the PB2 and PA endonuclease components capture capped fragments from host nascent RNAs, illustrating the cap-snatching mechanism used to initiate viral mRNA synthesis. Host chromatin and nuclear matrix factors are depicted nearby, suggesting that transcription occurs in a host nuclear environment rich in supporting proteins.\n\nThe lower part shows viral mRNAs emerging with a 5′ cap and poly(A) tail, alongside host factors involved in splicing and cap binding. Overall, the diagram emphasizes that influenza replication and transcription depend heavily on host nuclear import, transcription, RNA processing, and chromatin-associated factors.",
